<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  Entitlements for the embedded Swift helper (PR-042).

  NEVER APPLIED, and the binary it is written for HAS NEVER BEEN COMPILED.

  The helper is a separate executable at
  `Pilot.app/Contents/Resources/helper/PilotHelper`, spawned by the main
  process. It is the process that actually calls ScreenCaptureKit,
  `AXUIElement*`, `CGEventTapCreate`, `AVAudioEngine`, `SFSpeechRecognizer` and
  `AVSpeechSynthesizer` — so if macOS ever decides to attribute a TCC decision
  to *it* rather than to the app bundle, this is the file that has to be right.
  Which of the two happens is the oldest open structural risk in this project
  (recorded since PR-011); `docs/handoff.md` §1 step 22 settles it.

  It is a SEPARATE file from the app's, and deliberately narrower:

    device.audio-input   the microphone, which the helper opens itself.

  What is NOT here, and why:

    allow-jit / allow-unsigned-executable-memory
        Those exist for V8. The helper is compiled Swift with no interpreter in
        it, and granting a Swift process the right to write-then-execute memory
        would widen its attack surface for nothing. This is the reason the
        helper does not simply reuse the app's entitlements.

  The helper is signed with the SAME identity as the app (ad hoc today — see
  `apps/desktop/scripts/sign-mac.js`), because a differently-signed child is the
  configuration most likely to be treated by TCC as a separate subject.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
  <dict>
    <key>com.apple.security.device.audio-input</key>
    <true/>
  </dict>
</plist>
